# `Ẑ` is pro-cyclic

`Ẑ = NK.LocalAnabelian.Zhat`, the profinite completion of `ℤ`, is pro-cyclic in the sense of
`NK.LocalTame.IsProCyclic`: for **every** normal subgroup `N ≤ Ẑ` of finite index, the finite
quotient `Ẑ ⧸ N` is cyclic.

## Why this is not immediate

`IsProCyclic G` quantifies over *all* finite index normal subgroups, not only the *open* ones, and
a general profinite group has dense (hence non-open) finite index subgroups — an abstract
homomorphism from a profinite group to a finite group is *not* continuous in general.  For `Ẑ`
this cannot happen, and the reason is the divisibility of `Ẑ / ℤ`:

* `Ẑ = closure (Subgroup.zpowers g)` for `g = zEta 1` the canonical generator, so `Ẑ` is
  topologically generated by `g` (`zEta_denseRange`, `closure_zpowers_zhatGen`);
* `Ẑ` is commutative (`zhat_mul_comm`);
* for `n > 0`, the subgroup `powSubgroup n = {x ^ n}` of `n`-th powers is **closed** — it is the
  image of the (compact) `Ẑ` under the continuous power map (`isClosed_powSubgroup`);
* consequently `Ẑ ⧸ powSubgroup n` is a compact Hausdorff quotient of `Ẑ` in which the image of
  `g` is topologically generating with `g ^ n = 1`; its `zpowers` is finite, hence closed, hence
  everything: `Ẑ ⧸ powSubgroup n` is **cyclic** (`isCyclic_quotient_powSubgroup`).

The second point is what makes the argument uniform in `N`: for `N` of index `d`, the `d`-th
powers all lie in `N`, because in the finite quotient `Ẑ ⧸ N` (of order `d`) every element
satisfies `x ^ d = 1`.  Hence `powSubgroup d ≤ N`, so `Ẑ ⧸ N` is a quotient of the cyclic group
`Ẑ ⧸ powSubgroup d`, and quotients of cyclic groups are cyclic.

## Main result

* `NK.TowerRank.isProCyclic_zhat` : `NK.LocalTame.IsProCyclic NK.LocalAnabelian.Zhat`.
-/
